{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 7054 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d8cc3c103429e595f6bcb9f4d55a2184f87fe38e795406720af6a30054726c76" :edb/db-before-t 7053 :edb/db-after-t 7054 :edb/tx-data [[13194139540366 50 #inst "2026-10-25T06:57:53.327Z" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186167495 1071 "The table below gives the values of $Φ^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$, where $p = Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z \\le z) = 0.900$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.900$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.950$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.975$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.990$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.995$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9995$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Φ^{-1}(p)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2816$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.6449$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.9600$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.3263$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.5758$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.0902$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.2905$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186167495 1071 "The table below gives the values of $Φ^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$, where $p = Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z \\le z) = 0.900$.\n\n| $p$ | $0.900$ | $0.950$ | $0.975$ | $0.990$ | $0.995$ | $0.999$ | $0.9995$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $Φ^{-1}(p)$ | $1.2816$ | $1.6449$ | $1.9600$ | $2.3263$ | $2.5758$ | $3.0902$ | $3.2905$ |" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186167986 1064 "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 + y, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the modified Euler method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously completed the predictor step, resulting in the following values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe initially predicted new point produced by the predictor equation is \n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p)\n= (0+1,2)\n=(1,2).\n$$\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{p}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 3.$\n\nWe can view our approximation made by the modified Euler method in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/d0/d05c810ab241003432be9d17c7ca621e1aaca062fd6f649981b8aa63c4972935.png)\n\nNotice how the correction made by the corrector significantly improves the initial approximation produced by the predictor, at the cost of additional computation." 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186167986 1064 "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 + y, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the modified Euler method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously completed the predictor step, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nThe initially predicted new point produced by the predictor equation is \n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p)\n= (0+1,2)\n=(1,2).\n$$\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{p}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 3.$\n\nWe can view our approximation made by the modified Euler method in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/d0/d05c810ab241003432be9d17c7ca621e1aaca062fd6f649981b8aa63c4972935.png)\n\nNotice how the correction made by the corrector significantly improves the initial approximation produced by the predictor, at the cost of additional computation." 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186167989 1064 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x - 2y + 5, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nOur goal is to approximate $y(1)$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\nSince we know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1),$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nLet's use a table to keep track of all important values, including the intermediate slopes. We start by adding the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =3x-2y+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{'} & =3{x}_{1}-2{y}_{1}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{'} & =3{x}_{2}-2{y}_{2}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{'} & =3{x}_{3}-2{y}_{3}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 4+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{'}+2{y}_{1}^{'}+2{y}_{2}^{'}+{y}_{3}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 3+0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLooking at the two leftmost columns, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 3.$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186167989 1064 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x - 2y + 5, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nOur goal is to approximate $y(1)$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\nSince we know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1),$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nLet's use a table to keep track of all important values, including the intermediate slopes. We start by adding the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =3x-2y+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{'} & =3{x}_{1}-2{y}_{1}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{'} & =3{x}_{2}-2{y}_{2}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{'} & =3{x}_{3}-2{y}_{3}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 4+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{'}+2{y}_{1}^{'}+2{y}_{2}^{'}+{y}_{3}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 3+0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nLooking at the two leftmost columns, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 3.$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186167992 1064 "The **Modified Euler Method** (**Heun's Method**) is a predictor-corrector numerical method that uses Euler's method for its predictor step:\n\n$$\n\\text{Predictor}: \\qquad \\Delta y_p = y' \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The step size $\\Delta x$ represents the change in $x.$\n\n- The quantity $y'$ represents the slope at the current point.\n\n- The predicted $y$-increment is denoted $\\Delta y_p$ for notational convenience. It represents the initial change in $y$ made by the predictor step when using the modified Euler method.\n\nFor example, consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 + y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of using the modified Euler method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nSince we know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1),$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nWe'll use a table when applying the modified Euler method. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y_p\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\  & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y_p\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y_p\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y_p\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can view this initial Euler-based approximation produced by the predictor equation in the coordinate plane as follows.\n\n![](images/e5/e5466eaaa6cdf5805c79cb79ad8a1e6b5fb2cf02e789c8004d8b6ec3de95ea83.png)" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186167992 1064 "The **Modified Euler Method** (**Heun's Method**) is a predictor-corrector numerical method that uses Euler's method for its predictor step:\n\n$$\n\\text{Predictor}: \\qquad \\Delta y_p = y' \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The step size $\\Delta x$ represents the change in $x.$\n\n- The quantity $y'$ represents the slope at the current point.\n\n- The predicted $y$-increment is denoted $\\Delta y_p$ for notational convenience. It represents the initial change in $y$ made by the predictor step when using the modified Euler method.\n\nFor example, consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 + y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of using the modified Euler method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nSince we know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1),$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nWe'll use a table when applying the modified Euler method. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $\\,y_p\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\  & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $\\,y_p\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $\\,y_p\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $\\,y_p\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe can view this initial Euler-based approximation produced by the predictor equation in the coordinate plane as follows.\n\n![](images/e5/e5466eaaa6cdf5805c79cb79ad8a1e6b5fb2cf02e789c8004d8b6ec3de95ea83.png)" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168006 1064 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n$$\n\\lambda = 1, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{v} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{w} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}t \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.98$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.61$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.32$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186168006 1064 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n$$\n\\lambda = 1, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{v} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{w} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}t \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.30$ | $0$ | $3.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.61$ | $1$ | $3.32$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168008 1064 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=2x+y-z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=x+2y+z,\\, && z(0)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIs it possible to approximate solutions to initial value problems involving a system of equations, such as the one above, using Euler's method?\n\nIndeed, for a system of ODEs with initial values,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{'} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y = y(x), z = z(x),$ we can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by applying it to each ODE simultaneously, but separately:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\ Δz &={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's approximate the values of $y(1)$ and $z(1)$ for our initial value problem above using Euler's method with step size $\\Delta x = 1.$\n\nWe start by constructing a table that contains all relevant quantities, including slopes and increments for *both* variables.\nThen, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=2,$ we place $x=0,$ $y=1,$ and $z=2$ in the first row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we compute *both $y'$ and $z'$* according to the given rules using the current values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =2x+y-z\\, & \\,{z}^{'} & =x+2y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+1-2 &  & =0+2\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =-1 &  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\, & \\,Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1 &  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1 &  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+4=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 0$ and $z(1) \\approx 6.$\n\nNotice that this component-wise approach is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a system of ODEs!" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186168008 1064 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=2x+y-z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=x+2y+z,\\, && z(0)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIs it possible to approximate solutions to initial value problems involving a system of equations, such as the one above, using Euler's method?\n\nIndeed, for a system of ODEs with initial values,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{'} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y = y(x), z = z(x),$ we can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by applying it to each ODE simultaneously, but separately:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\ Δz &={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's approximate the values of $y(1)$ and $z(1)$ for our initial value problem above using Euler's method with step size $\\Delta x = 1.$\n\nWe start by constructing a table that contains all relevant quantities, including slopes and increments for *both* variables.\nThen, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=2,$ we place $x=0,$ $y=1,$ and $z=2$ in the first row.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute *both $y'$ and $z'$* according to the given rules using the current values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =2x+y-z\\, & \\,{z}^{'} & =x+2y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+1-2 &  & =0+2\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =-1 &  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\, & \\,Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1 &  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1 &  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+4=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $6$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 0$ and $z(1) \\approx 6.$\n\nNotice that this component-wise approach is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a system of ODEs!" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168010 1064 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\cos   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\sin   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sin   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\cos   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R},\n$$\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.22$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186168010 1064 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\cos   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\sin   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sin   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\cos   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R},\n$$\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.78$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.78$ | $1.22$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168033 1064 "Let's go back to our previous initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x-2y+5, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nCarrying out the Runge-Kutta method resulted in the following table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can view each step of our approximation in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n- The first intermediate point $$\n\\displaystyle (x_1, y_1) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac52\\right)\n$$ is found using the slope of the initial point $y'=3.$\n\n![](images/ca/cabf5d9d55f4840f2df6d90fbeb304c5235f98cd67647903ebd1f59c6a3819cc.png)\n\n- The second intermediate point $$\n\\displaystyle (x_2, y_2) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac74\\right)\n$$ is found using the slope of the previous intermediate point $y'_1=\\dfrac32.$\n\n![](images/21/213e765c0f866b24028bdddbe1f3c709bdfec83b5e84bbe9ec9b97906f50970a.png)\n\n- The third intermediate point $(x_3, y_3) = (1,4)$ is found using the slope of the previous intermediate point $y'_2=3.$\n\n![](images/76/769e51f64f8ba9a0c2c0bc8ae648e33e33ff4d4ea5bc6fbd73ddfdfad1d05481.png)\n\n- Finally, we compute the increment in $y$ by taking a weighted average of the four sample slopes.\n\n![](images/c6/c6b5f34e6cc55733c2dfdc2faeb0333c9b214d54a88abc0baea801dae3764a3f.png)" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186168033 1064 "Let's go back to our previous initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x-2y+5, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nCarrying out the Runge-Kutta method resulted in the following table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nWe can view each step of our approximation in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n- The first intermediate point $$\n\\displaystyle (x_1, y_1) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac52\\right)\n$$ is found using the slope of the initial point $y'=3.$\n\n![](images/ca/cabf5d9d55f4840f2df6d90fbeb304c5235f98cd67647903ebd1f59c6a3819cc.png)\n\n- The second intermediate point $$\n\\displaystyle (x_2, y_2) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac74\\right)\n$$ is found using the slope of the previous intermediate point $y'_1=\\dfrac32.$\n\n![](images/21/213e765c0f866b24028bdddbe1f3c709bdfec83b5e84bbe9ec9b97906f50970a.png)\n\n- The third intermediate point $(x_3, y_3) = (1,4)$ is found using the slope of the previous intermediate point $y'_2=3.$\n\n![](images/76/769e51f64f8ba9a0c2c0bc8ae648e33e33ff4d4ea5bc6fbd73ddfdfad1d05481.png)\n\n- Finally, we compute the increment in $y$ by taking a weighted average of the four sample slopes.\n\n![](images/c6/c6b5f34e6cc55733c2dfdc2faeb0333c9b214d54a88abc0baea801dae3764a3f.png)" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168066 1064 "When working with normally distributed random variables, we often want to find a value of $z$ that gives rise to a specific probability. \n\nFor example, suppose that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to find the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z\\leq z) = 0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem is to find a value of $z$ such that $\\Phi(z) = 0.85.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc2006ce351b075c735f9fc1ace31d5688cd39639a6c526810011b44e631be44.png)\n\nA good place to start is the CDF table for $\\Phi(z)$ around the $z$-values where $\\Phi(z)\\approx 0.85.$ This part of the table is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.04 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.05 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.0 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8485 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8508 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8531 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.1 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8708 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8729 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8749 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.2 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8907 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8925 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8944 \\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere is no listed value of $z$ where $\\Phi(z) = 0.85$ exactly. However, notice that there are values where $\\Phi(z)$ is slightly below and slightly above $0.85{:}$\n\n- $\\Phi(1.03) = 0.8485 < 0.85$\n\n- $\\Phi(1.04) = 0.8508 > 0.85$\n\nTherefore, the required value of $z$ must lie between $1.03$ and $1.04.$ To approximate this value of $z,$ we take the average of these two numbers:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{1.03 + 1.04}{2} = 1.035\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.035) \\approx 0.85.\n$$\n\nTaking the average of two numbers on an interval to find an improved approximation is known as **interval bisection**." 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186168066 1064 "When working with normally distributed random variables, we often want to find a value of $z$ that gives rise to a specific probability. \n\nFor example, suppose that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to find the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z\\leq z) = 0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem is to find a value of $z$ such that $\\Phi(z) = 0.85.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc2006ce351b075c735f9fc1ace31d5688cd39639a6c526810011b44e631be44.png)\n\nA good place to start is the CDF table for $\\Phi(z)$ around the $z$-values where $\\Phi(z)\\approx 0.85.$ This part of the table is shown below.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8485 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8508 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8531 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8708 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8729 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8749 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8907 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8925 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8944 \\,$ |\n\nThere is no listed value of $z$ where $\\Phi(z) = 0.85$ exactly. However, notice that there are values where $\\Phi(z)$ is slightly below and slightly above $0.85{:}$\n\n- $\\Phi(1.03) = 0.8485 < 0.85$\n\n- $\\Phi(1.04) = 0.8508 > 0.85$\n\nTherefore, the required value of $z$ must lie between $1.03$ and $1.04.$ To approximate this value of $z,$ we take the average of these two numbers:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{1.03 + 1.04}{2} = 1.035\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.035) \\approx 0.85.\n$$\n\nTaking the average of two numbers on an interval to find an improved approximation is known as **interval bisection**." 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168068 1064 "We may be given a slope field without knowing the differential equation that produced it. So, what properties of a differential equation can we deduce from its slope field?\n\nAs an example, consider the slope field of the differential equation $y' = x(3-x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35c80fb123a13c6bdd24c46cc5dccc03a9b4c37f32924b822c5679b109742951.png)\n\nNow, imagine that we don't know which differential equation produced this slope field. Our goal, then, is to gather as much information as possible about the equation by referring *only* to its slope field. \n\nWhen analyzing slope fields, it's often helpful to visualize a few of the solution curves, so let's sketch a few.\n\n![](images/eb/eb3757046fe4d6b0d19fbc373f2c2d01022498021cb671640683d9b400763d57.png)\n\nWe can now make the following observations:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical symmetry. Therefore, the equation must be of the form $y' = f(x).$ Indeed, the equation $y' = x(3-x)$ is of this form.\n\n- Second, notice that the solution curves have stationary points along the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ This means that the right-hand side of our differential equation *must evaluate to zero* on these lines. Indeed, this is true of our equation.\n\n![](images/7e/7eacb2992b0720a4de96c1f916417bd8a23eeb72f3b26753f4ab4debacec5335.png)\n\n- Third, notice that the lines $x=0$ and $x=3$ split the plane into three regions. The slopes of the solution curves in each of these regions are summarized in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\"><tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Region</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x <  0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$0 <  x <  3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x > 3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Slope</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sign</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe equation $y' = x(3-x)$ indeed exhibits this behavior. To see this a little more clearly, let $f(x) = x(3-x).$ A sketch of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6f0b8309e57703d2f18dce614b59eb8f853c0320ba12f964b8c9a4dd50e0e9f.png)\n\n The sign of $f(x)$ in the three regions agrees with the above table:\n\n- $f(x)$ is positive for $0 < x < 3,$ and\n\n- $f(x)$ is negative for $x<0$ and $x >3.$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186168068 1064 "We may be given a slope field without knowing the differential equation that produced it. So, what properties of a differential equation can we deduce from its slope field?\n\nAs an example, consider the slope field of the differential equation $y' = x(3-x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35c80fb123a13c6bdd24c46cc5dccc03a9b4c37f32924b822c5679b109742951.png)\n\nNow, imagine that we don't know which differential equation produced this slope field. Our goal, then, is to gather as much information as possible about the equation by referring *only* to its slope field. \n\nWhen analyzing slope fields, it's often helpful to visualize a few of the solution curves, so let's sketch a few.\n\n![](images/eb/eb3757046fe4d6b0d19fbc373f2c2d01022498021cb671640683d9b400763d57.png)\n\nWe can now make the following observations:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical symmetry. Therefore, the equation must be of the form $y' = f(x).$ Indeed, the equation $y' = x(3-x)$ is of this form.\n\n- Second, notice that the solution curves have stationary points along the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ This means that the right-hand side of our differential equation *must evaluate to zero* on these lines. Indeed, this is true of our equation.\n\n![](images/7e/7eacb2992b0720a4de96c1f916417bd8a23eeb72f3b26753f4ab4debacec5335.png)\n\n- Third, notice that the lines $x=0$ and $x=3$ split the plane into three regions. The slopes of the solution curves in each of these regions are summarized in the table below.\n\n| Region | $x <  0$ | $0 <  x <  3$ | $x > 3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nThe equation $y' = x(3-x)$ indeed exhibits this behavior. To see this a little more clearly, let $f(x) = x(3-x).$ A sketch of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6f0b8309e57703d2f18dce614b59eb8f853c0320ba12f964b8c9a4dd50e0e9f.png)\n\n The sign of $f(x)$ in the three regions agrees with the above table:\n\n- $f(x)$ is positive for $0 < x < 3,$ and\n\n- $f(x)$ is negative for $x<0$ and $x >3.$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168074 1064 "Let's consider the differential equation $y' = 2y(1-y).$ The slope field for this equation is shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b53d7d2a486719ea787ce84f5d2b18de656dd1013625914af740e2f4db7e1319.png)\n\nLet's see how much information we can gather about the differential equation by referring *only* to its slope field.\n\n- First, note that the slope field has horizontal symmetry. Therefore, the associated differential equation must be of the form $y' = f(y)$ (i.e., it must be autonomous). Indeed, the equation $y' = 2y(1-y)$ is autonomous.\n\n- Second, recall that $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of an autonomous differential equation $y' = f(y)$ if $f(c) = 0.$ Equilibrium solutions correspond to horizontal solution curves.\n\nIn this case, there appear to be two equilibrium solutions, $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ Let's highlight these equilibrium solutions in our slope field.\n\n![](images/88/88dc3158b363fa2ea3c0ff40f51527a5c5184f7c05ea84b5a66e7225eb64ef11.png)\n\n \nNote that $y=0$ and $y=1$ are indeed equilibrium solutions of $y' = 2y(1-y)$ since the right-hand side evaluates to zero on these lines.\n\n- Third, the lines $y=0$ and $y=1$ split the plane into three regions. Let's sketch some solution curves, one for each region. \n\n![](images/aa/aa0e226f416c8399f9e8e37c090c28a53e670a0018e8010034a2bf2575560420.png)\n\n \nThe slopes of the solution curves in each region are summarized in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Region</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$y <  0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$0 <  y <  1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$y > 1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Slope</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sign</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n  The equation $y' = 2y(1-y)$ indeed exhibits this behavior. To see this a little more clearly, let $f(y) = 2y(1-y).$ A sketch of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e1d9b61a9dd6d483080d52675dd7bebab0a14dd394e6a301bf8c92727eb0257.png)\n\n  Notice that the sign of $f(y)$ in the three regions agrees with the above table:\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $0 < y < 1,$ and\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y<0$ and $y >1.$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186168074 1064 "Let's consider the differential equation $y' = 2y(1-y).$ The slope field for this equation is shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b53d7d2a486719ea787ce84f5d2b18de656dd1013625914af740e2f4db7e1319.png)\n\nLet's see how much information we can gather about the differential equation by referring *only* to its slope field.\n\n- First, note that the slope field has horizontal symmetry. Therefore, the associated differential equation must be of the form $y' = f(y)$ (i.e., it must be autonomous). Indeed, the equation $y' = 2y(1-y)$ is autonomous.\n\n- Second, recall that $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of an autonomous differential equation $y' = f(y)$ if $f(c) = 0.$ Equilibrium solutions correspond to horizontal solution curves.\n\nIn this case, there appear to be two equilibrium solutions, $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ Let's highlight these equilibrium solutions in our slope field.\n\n![](images/88/88dc3158b363fa2ea3c0ff40f51527a5c5184f7c05ea84b5a66e7225eb64ef11.png)\n\n \nNote that $y=0$ and $y=1$ are indeed equilibrium solutions of $y' = 2y(1-y)$ since the right-hand side evaluates to zero on these lines.\n\n- Third, the lines $y=0$ and $y=1$ split the plane into three regions. Let's sketch some solution curves, one for each region. \n\n![](images/aa/aa0e226f416c8399f9e8e37c090c28a53e670a0018e8010034a2bf2575560420.png)\n\n \nThe slopes of the solution curves in each region are summarized in the table below.\n\n| Region | $y <  0$ | $0 <  y <  1$ | $y > 1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n  The equation $y' = 2y(1-y)$ indeed exhibits this behavior. To see this a little more clearly, let $f(y) = 2y(1-y).$ A sketch of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e1d9b61a9dd6d483080d52675dd7bebab0a14dd394e6a301bf8c92727eb0257.png)\n\n  Notice that the sign of $f(y)$ in the three regions agrees with the above table:\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $0 < y < 1,$ and\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y<0$ and $y >1.$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168083 1064 "One final way to find the $z$-score for a given probability is to simply look it up in a **percentage points table**, such as the one below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\quad p \\quad$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.55$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.60$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.85$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.95$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Phi^{-1}(p)$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.1257\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.2533\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.3853\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.5244\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.6745\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.8416\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.0364\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.2816\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.6449\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis table gives the values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.\n\nLet's use the table above to find $z$ such that $P(Z \\leq z) = 0.75.$\nFirst, we express the given information in terms of $\\Phi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le z) & =0.75 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.75,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np = 0.75 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(p) = 0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75) = 0.6745.\n$$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186168083 1064 "One final way to find the $z$-score for a given probability is to simply look it up in a **percentage points table**, such as the one below.\n\n| $\\quad p \\quad$ | $0.55$ | $0.60$ | $0.65$ | $0.70$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.1257\\,$ | $\\,0.2533\\,$ | $\\,0.3853\\,$ | $\\,0.5244\\,$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\nThis table gives the values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.\n\nLet's use the table above to find $z$ such that $P(Z \\leq z) = 0.75.$\nFirst, we express the given information in terms of $\\Phi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le z) & =0.75 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.75,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np = 0.75 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(p) = 0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75) = 0.6745.\n$$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186168126 1064 "The **hyperbolic secant** is the reciprocal of the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n$$\ny = \\sech{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh{x}} = \\dfrac{2}{e^x+e^{-x}}\n$$\n\nTo plot the graph of $y=\\sech x$, we create a table with some values, rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n