\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.282$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.645$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.960$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.326$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.576$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186171692 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=45$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma=7$. We wish to carry out a hypothesis test at a $5\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu$ is smaller than $18$.\n\nGiven that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=17$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The **one-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\leq -2.576$\n2. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\lt 18$\n3. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\lt 18$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186171692 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample means is also normal, namely,\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu = 18$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu \\lt 18$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e., $\\mu=18$, we compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x^(―) - μ)/(σ/\\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{17 - 18}{7/\\sqrt{45}} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.958\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the table, the $5\\%$ one-tailed critical value is $z \\approx 1.645$. \n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.282$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\color{blue}1.645$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.960$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.326$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.576$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\nHowever, since the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu \\lt 18$, we must consider the left tail. We do this using the symmetry of the normal distribution. So, our critical region is $Z \\leq -1.645$.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Our test statistic ($-0.958$) does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/d1/d15