$) does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/ae/ae51f764e4530a60172fdc7216555d975d1b3c5f30ccca6c7a244572333bba4f.png)\nSo, we can't reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:$\\qquad$ *There is **insufficient** evidence that, at the $10\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu < 189$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186171704 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance $\\sigma^2$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 40 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu = 189$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu < 189$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = 187 \\, \\textrm{mg/dL}, \\qquad n = 40, \\qquad s = 12 \\, \\textrm{mg/dL}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e., $\\mu=189$, we compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - μ)/(s/\\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{187 - 189}{12/\\sqrt{40}} \\\\\n&\\approx - 1.054\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the table, the $10\\%$ one-tailed critical value for a $t$-distributed random variable with $n-1=40-1=39$ degrees of freedom is $t \\approx 1.304$.\n$k \\setminus p$$0.100$$0.050$$0.025$$0.010$$0.005$$39$$\\color{blue}\\,1.304\\,$ $\\,1.685\\,$ $\\,2.023\\,$ $\\,2.426\\,$ $\\,2.708\\,$ $40$$\\,1.303\\,$ $\\,1.684\\,$ $\\,2.021\\,$ $\\,2.423\\,$ $\\,2.704\\,$ $41$$\\,1.303\\,$ $\\,1.683\\,$ $\\,2.020\\,$ $\\,2.421\\,$ $\\,2.701\\,$\nSince the alternative hypothesis is $\\m