}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 3.193\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the table, the $1\\%$ *two-tailed* critical value (the same as the $1\\% \\div 2 = 0.5\\%$ *one-tailed* critical value) for $\\nu=46$ degrees of freedom is $t \\approx 2.687$.\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$44$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.301\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,1.680\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,2.015\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,2.414\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,2.692\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$45$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.301\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,1.679\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,2.014\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,2.412\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,2.690\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$46$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.300\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,1.679\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,2.013\\,$ </td>\n<td>$\\,2.410\\,$ </td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\color{blue}\\,2.687\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we are considering both tails, our critical region is\n$T \\leq -2.687 \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad T \\geq 2.687$.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Our test statistic ($-3.193$) lies in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/56/560aa112f663962805c8a28d557a21169e330296d44e207ea941dafb67e1f360.png)\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is **sufficient** evidence that, at the $1\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186171708 1074 "We are told that the populations are normally distributed and their variances are equal, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{S_p \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_\\nu$ with\n\n$$\n\\nu=n_x+n_y-2\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes,\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances, and\n- $S_p^2 = \\dfrac{(n_x-1)S_x^2+(n_y-1)S_y^2}{n_x+n_y-2}$ is the pooled estimate of the variance.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$ is the alternative (two-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = 6, \\quad \\overline{y} = 7.5, \\quad n_x = 22, \\quad n_y = 26, \\quad s_x = 1, \\quad s_y = 2\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the number of degrees of freedom and the pooled estimate of the variance for our samples:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= 22 + 26 - 2 = 46 \\\\\nS_{p}^{2} &= ((22 - 1)(1)^{2} + (26 - 1)(2)^{2})/(22 + 26 - 2) = \\frac{121}{46}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\mu_x-\\mu_y=0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(S_{p}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n_{x}} + \\frac{1}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (6 - 7.5 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{121}{46}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{22} + \\frac{1}{26}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 3.193\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the table, the $1\\%$ *two-tailed* critical value (the same as the $1\\% \\div 2 = 0.5\\%$ *one-tailed* critical value) for $\\nu=46$ degrees of freedom is $t \\approx 2.687$.\n$k \\setminus p$$0.100$$0.050$$0.025$$0.010$$0.005$$44$$\\,1.301\\,$ $\\,1.680\\,$ $\\,2.015\\,$ $\\,2.414\\,$ $\\,2.692\\,$ $45$$\\,1.301\\,$ $\\,1.679\\,$ $\\,2.014\\,$ $\\,2.412\\,$ $\\,2.690\\,$ $46$$\\,1.300\\,$ $\\,1.679\\,$ $\\,2.013\\,$ $\\,2.410\\,$ $\\color{blue}\\,2.687\\,$\nSince we are considering both tails, our critical region is\n$T \\leq -2.687 \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad T \\geq 2.687$.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Our test statistic ($-3.193$) lies in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/56/560aa112f663962805c8a28d557a21169e330296d44e207ea941dafb67e1f360.png)\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is **sufficient** evidence that, at the $1\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186171709 1071 "The diameters of balls for bearings produced by machines $X$ and $Y$ have standard deviations of $\\sigma_x = 0.2\\,\\text{mm}$ and $\\sigma_y = 0.3\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively. Engineers want to know whether the population mean $\\mu_x$ for the diameter of balls produced by machine $X$ is larger than the population mean $\\mu_y$ for the diameter of balls produced by machine $Y.$ They construct a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.   \n\nGiven that the engineers sampled $100$ balls produced by machine $X$ and $150$ balls made by machine $Y$ and found that their mean diameters are $10.05\\,\\text{mm}$ and $10.01\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.04 \\pm  0.081.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99\\%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that $\\mu_x > \\mu_y.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.282$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.645$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.960$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.326$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.576$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186171709 1071 "The diameters of balls for bearings produced by machines $X$ and $Y$ have standard deviations of $\\sigma_x = 0.2\\,\\text{mm}$ and $\\sigma_y = 0.3\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively. Engineers want to know whether the population mean $\\mu_x$ for the diameter of balls produced by machine $X$ is larger than the population mean $\\mu_y$ for the diameter of balls produced by machine $Y.$ They construct a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.   \n\nGiven that the engineers sampled $100$ balls produced by machine $X$ and $150$ balls made by machine $Y$ and found that their mean diameters are $10.05\\,\\text{mm}$ and $10.01\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.04 \\pm  0.081.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99\\%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that $\\mu_x > \\mu_y.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186171709 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\geq30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances,  $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$$\n\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 10.05-10.01 = 0.04\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005.\n$$\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z > z_{0.005}) = 0.005,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005} = 2.576 :$ \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.005}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.282$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.645$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.960$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.326$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.576}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$$\nE = 2.576 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.2^2}{100}+\\dfrac{0.3^2}{150}} \\approx 0.081\n$$ \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle 0.04 \\pm  0.081\\end{array}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.04 \\pm  0.081.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains zero.\n$$\n(0.04 - 0.081, \\, 0.04 + 0.081) = (-0.041, \\, 0.121)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that $\\mu_x = \\mu_y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186171709 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\geq30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances,  $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$$\n\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 10.05-10.01 = 0.04\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005.\n$$\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z > z_{0.005}) = 0.005,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005} = 2.576 :$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.005}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.576}$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$$\nE = 2.576 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.2^2}{100}+\\dfrac{0.3^2}{150}} \\approx 0.081\n$$ \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle 0.04 \\pm  0.081\\end{array}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.04 \\pm  0.081.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains zero.\n$$\n(0.04 - 0.081, \\, 0.04 + 0.081) = (-0.041, \\, 0.121)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that $\\mu_x = \\mu_y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186171716 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) =   x-y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2 + z^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -1 &=\\lambda \\\\ -1 &=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=-1 \\\\ \\mu z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu$ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $z= 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2, 0, 0), \\qquad (-2, 4, 0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$(x,y,z)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2, 0, 0 )$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-2, 4, 0)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x,y,z)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2, 4, 0),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186171716 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) =   x-y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2 + z^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -1 &=\\lambda \\\\ -1 &=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=-1 \\\\ \\mu z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu$ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $z= 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2, 0, 0), \\qquad (-2, 4, 0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2, 0, 0 )$ | $(-2, 4, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $2$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2, 4, 0),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$" 13194139540366 17592186576411 false] [17592186171719 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-2-(-7) \\\\  & =3-(-2) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-7+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-7+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{5n-12 \\mid \\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$B$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$A$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5n-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in B,$ we have $f(n) = 5n-12 \\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139540366 17592186576411 true] [17592186171719 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-2-(-7) \\\\  & =3-(-2) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-7+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-7+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{5n-12 \\mid \\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\cdots$ | $5n-12$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in B,$ we have $f(n) = 5n-12 \\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. 